From f1fb52d592fd29260fa82d8a1b4cc885d0846b97 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 03:25:15 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(kanban): recover an abandoned updater pause only for its
 own attempt [daveey]

Review of 3c94b431 (t_67e13d17bcd768f9) found that recover_and_drain checked
the saved intent against a task snapshot, then called kb.reclaim_task with only
the task id. That call reselects whatever attempt is current. If observer A
stalls after its matching read while observer B finishes the same pause and the
dispatcher starts a replacement, A fences, signals and closes the replacement.

Recovery now adopts the exact intent atomically: under one write transaction it
requires the same token, the same abandoned owner and a task row still matching
the intent's run/claim/PID/start identity in `running`, and moves ownership to
itself. Otherwise it is a no-op and never creates a fresh command for the
current run. It then finishes the pause through _reclaim with the intent's own
identity: termination targets the intent's PID + fingerprint and the close is a
CAS on that identity, both outside the adoption transaction. The fence's
unwind/cleanup is shared as _settle, so an adopted pause that unwinds stays
durable for the next observer.

RED at 3c94b431 with only the new test:
test_stale_recovery_never_touches_a_replacement_attempt[finished|replaced]
2 failed, "replacement worker was signalled by stale recovery; run 2 ready".
GREEN: 7 passed in the recovery file; 89 reclaim/crash-sweep files,
1758 passed, 0 failed, 16 skipped.
